# And I found what may be the architectural clue

The new `/.well-known/ai` proposal is especially interesting because it formalizes something I've been circling around in this answer: **machine-readable service discovery at the web level**. ([IETF Datatracker][8])

Imagine you crawl:

```text
MCP Registry
A2A Agent Cards
OpenAPI
/.well-known/ai
ai.txt
robots.txt
x402 services
GitHub
provider documentation
pricing pages
status pages
benchmark results
regulatory sources
```

into **one graph**.

Then your products aren't actually separate products.

```text
                    ENTITY
                      │
       ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
       ↓              ↓              ↓
      API            TOOL           AGENT
       │              │              │
       └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                      ↓
                  CAPABILITY
                      │
     ┌───────┬────────┼──────┬────────┐
     ↓       ↓        ↓      ↓        ↓
   PRICE   TRUST   QUALITY  POLICY  STATUS
     │       │        │      │        │
     └───────┴────────┴──────┴────────┘
                      ↓
                  DECISION
```

You could release products almost mechanically:

```text
/week 1   prices API
/week 2   deals API
/week 3   x402 index
/week 4   MCP health
/week 5   MCP diffs
/week 6   AI rights
/week 7   API alternatives
/week 8   provenance
...
```

while continuously enriching **the same underlying intelligence graph**.

## The first five I'd actually ship

**1. LLM/API Deals + Pricing** — easiest wedge, immediately useful, obvious affiliate/data/subscription monetization.

**2. `/.well-known/ai` + MCP + A2A + x402 capability index** — get in ridiculously early on machine-service discovery. The underlying standards are appearing *right now*. ([IETF Datatracker][8])

**3. MCP/API Health + Schema Drift** — objective data, recurring need, cheap infrastructure, becomes more valuable with history.

**4. AI Rights/Terms API** — `robots + ai.txt + licenses + TOS → can_agent_do(X)?`. Very interesting intersection of agents, content and compliance. ([IETF Datatracker][9])

**5. Machine Commerce Intelligence** — normalize x402 services, pricing, reliability and capability, then eventually build `POST /should_buy`. Coinbase's infrastructure now explicitly allows agents to discover and pay for services automatically, so the missing layer is increasingly **selection intelligence**, not payment plumbing. ([Coinbase Developer Documentation][17])

The strongest meta-strategy is therefore **not “batch produce random APIs.”** It is: **batch-produce narrow intelligence products that all contribute observations to one historical Agent Intelligence Graph.** Each little free API can attract integrations; collectively they build a dataset that's much harder to replicate than any individual endpoint.

